Chapter 1

Introduction and Motivation

The vision of a self‑sustained starship carrying thousands of humans to another star system has captured the imagination of engineers and philosophers for over a century. In recent decades, the concept has been refined under names such as Worldship, Vaisseau‑Monde, or Generation Ship. The original Vaisseau‑Monde OSS study assumed propulsion breakthroughs—fusion drives, antimatter rockets—that would permit transit times on the order of a human lifetime. Yet as of 2026, none of these technologies exist beyond the conceptual stage; they are not on a credible engineering roadmap, and they violate the fundamental principle of designing only with what is demonstrably feasible.

This dissertation takes the opposite approach. It asks: What kind of vessel can we design using only propulsion, life support, materials, and sensor systems that are real in 2026—or are direct, conservative extrapolations of existing technology? The answer that emerges is not a fast starship but a closed civilisational system that must function without external support for a period longer than all of recorded human history.

Under nuclear‑electric propulsion with realistic specific impulse (2 500–5 000 s) and structural mass ratios, the cruise velocity to Proxima Centauri is bounded between 5 and 39 km s⁻¹, yielding a transit time of 32 700 to 254 000 years. At a nominal 25‑year generation, this corresponds to 1 200 to 10 000 generations in complete isolation. No existing generation‑ship study addresses timescales of this magnitude; the engineering, biological, social, and informational challenges are qualitatively different. The central thesis of this work is that such a vessel can only be conceived if it is re‑architected as a permanent deep‑space civilisation, with the interstellar destination becoming a secondary, conditional goal.

To ground the analysis in reality, we introduce a strict 2026 Admissible Technology Set A_{2026}. Every theorem proved in this dissertation operates within its constraints. Any parameter outside A_{2026} must be flagged as CONJECTURE and cannot be used to prove feasibility.

The dissertation proceeds in three major phases. First, we build the mathematical spine: a set of formal theorems that establish hard bounds on velocity, population size, life‑support closure, governance stability, knowledge fidelity, sensor performance, and cost. These proofs constitute the backbone of the Slow Ship Constitution. Second, we synthesise these results to demonstrate that a Proxima‑bound mission under A_{2026} is architecturally unsound, and we propose instead a Phase I mission to the Oort Cloud or a free‑floating deep‑space habitat—an achievable goal within the proven bounds. Third, we lay out a comprehensive research programme (Proposals 1–7) to close the remaining open problems, transforming the Constitution from a set of theoretical bounds into a fully validated engineering specification.

This dissertation is not a finished design; it is a rigorous theoretical foundation upon which such a design could be built. The proofs presented here are intended to be the minimum necessary to eliminate the most dangerous fallacies that have plagued earlier worldship concepts: the fallacy of rapid transit, the fallacy of small founding populations, the fallacy of perfect recycling, the fallacy of immutable governance, and the fallacy of linear cost scaling. By refuting these, we clear the ground for a new, honest architecture.

