Chapter 2

The 2026 Grounding Rule: Admissible Technology Set A_{2026}

All theorems and claims in this dissertation are subject to the following definition.

Definition 2.1 — Admissible Technology Set A_{2026}.
The following technologies and performance bounds are considered admissible for proving feasibility. Extrapolations are permissible only where explicitly noted; no parameter may exceed the stated bounds without being flagged as CONJECTURE.

1. Propulsion: Hall‑effect or gridded ion thrusters, specific impulse I_{sp} \in [2500, 5000] s. Technology Readiness Level (TRL) 4–6 at kW‑scale; MW‑class extrapolation is credible but noted.
2. Power: Fission reactors, specific mass \alpha = 10\text{–}30 kg kW⁻¹. MW‑to‑GW power levels are extrapolations from existing naval and space reactor programmes.
3. Materials: No room‑temperature superconductors, no active support structures (e.g., dynamic compression members), no antimatter. Structural mass fractions from known aerospace alloys and composites (e.g., Al‑Li, carbon fibre).
4. Life Support: Upper recycling efficiencies bounded by the best demonstrated closed‑loop systems: MELISSA (ESA) and BIOS‑3, with per‑cycle efficiencies of 90–98% for water, oxygen, and carbon loops. No system has demonstrated >98% closure for multi‑year durations.

Any design feature requiring a technology outside A_{2026} is not disallowed but must be explicitly marked as CONJECTURE and cannot serve as the basis for a feasibility proof. The purpose of this rule is to ensure that every “it is possible” claim is backed by hardware that exists today or is a straightforward engineering scale‑up.

