Chapter 3

Propulsion and the Velocity Ceiling

3.1 Velocity Ceiling Lemma (T1.1)

Statement.
For any worldship propelled by thrusters with specific impulse I_{sp} \in [2500, 5000] s and a total vehicle mass ratio R = m_0/m_f \in [1.5, 5.0], the maximum achievable mission \Delta v is bounded by 79 km s⁻¹, and the steady cruise velocity lies between 5 and 39 km s⁻¹.

Proof.
Exhaust velocity is given by v_e = g_0 I_{sp}, with g_0 = 9.81\ \text{m s}^{-2}.
For I_{sp}=2500 s, v_e = 24.5\ \text{km s}^{-1}; for I_{sp}=5000 s, v_e = 49.0\ \text{km s}^{-1}.

The ideal rocket equation (Tsiolkovsky) yields the total change in velocity:

\Delta v = v_e \ln R.
\]  

Using the admissible R range,

\min \Delta v = 24.5 \cdot \ln(1.5) \approx 9.9\ \text{km s}^{-1}, \qquad
\max \Delta v = 49.0 \cdot \ln(5.0) \approx 79.0\ \text{km s}^{-1}.

A fraction of \Delta v must be reserved for gravity losses, mid‑course corrections, and terminal deceleration. Realistic mission design allocates 50–80 % of the total \Delta v to the cruise phase. Hence,

v_{\text{cruise}} \in [0.5 \times 9.9,\; 0.8 \times 79] \approx [5,\; 39]\ \text{km s}^{-1}.

The upper bound on R (R \le 5.0) is dictated by structural constraints. A worldship comprises a massive habitat (hundreds of thousands of tonnes of structure, shielding, and life support) and a propulsion system; the propellant mass fraction cannot be arbitrarily large without violating the strength of materials in A_{2026}. A detailed mass budget (Chapter 5) confirms that R > 5 would require fuel mass exceeding four times the dry mass, incompatible with known aerospace structural fractions. ∎

3.2 Transit Time Theorem (T1.2)

Statement.
For a voyage to Proxima Centauri (distance D = 4.24\ \text{ly} = 4.01 \times 10^{13} km) at cruise velocity v_{\text{cruise}} \in [5,39] km s⁻¹, the one‑way transit time T lies in the interval [32\,700,\; 254\,000] years.

Proof.
The transit time is simply T = D / v_{\text{cruise}}.
Lower bound: T_{\min} = \frac{4.01 \times 10^{13}}{39} \approx 1.03 \times 10^{12}\ \text{s} = 32\,700\ \text{years}.
Upper bound: T_{\max} = \frac{4.01 \times 10^{13}}{5} \approx 8.02 \times 10^{12}\ \text{s} = 254\,000\ \text{years}.
(One year = 3.156 \times 10^7 s.)
Acceleration time under NEP at MW–GW power levels is on the order of decades, which is negligible relative to the total transit. ∎

Open Problem 3.1 (T1.3 – Multi‑Burn Optimisation).
Is it possible to increase the effective cruise velocity beyond v_e \ln R by pre‑deploying fission fuel depots along the trajectory or by using gravity assists?
Formally, one must solve

\max \sum \Delta v_i \quad \text{subject to} \quad \sum m_{\text{prop},i} \le M_{\text{fuel}},\; P_{\text{reactor}} \le P_{\max},
\]  

and show that the solution yields a higher arrival speed than a single‑burn profile. Preliminary analysis: interstellar space contains no massive bodies for gravity assists beyond the Oort Cloud; Oberth manoeuvres require a deep gravity well, which is absent. Any fuel depots must themselves be accelerated to the same velocity, offsetting the gain. Under A_{2026}, this remains an open problem; no proof of a significant gain currently exists.

