Chapter 4

Population Genetics Under Multi‑Millennial Isolation

We model the worldship population as a randomly mating diploid population of constant census size N, with effective population size N_e. The number of generations is t = T/25, giving t \in [1200, 10\,000].

4.1 Inbreeding Coefficient and Minimum Founder Bound (T2.1)

Statement.
To keep the inbreeding coefficient F_t < 0.1 after t generations, the required effective population size satisfies N_e \ge 47\,500 for t = 10\,000. A founding census population of 40 000–50 000 is the minimum defensible bound under A_{2026}.

Proof.
Under the Wright–Fisher model with no mutation or migration, the inbreeding coefficient evolves as

F_t = \frac{1}{2N_e} + \left(1 - \frac{1}{2N_e}\right) F_{t-1},\quad F_0 = 0.
\]  

The closed‑form solution is

F_t = 1 - \left(1 - \frac{1}{2N_e}\right)^t.
\]  

We require F_t < 0.1, i.e.,

\left(1 - \frac{1}{2N_e}\right)^t > 0.9.
\]  

Taking natural logarithms and using the approximation \ln(1 - x) \approx -x (valid for large N_e),

t \cdot \left(-\frac{1}{2N_e}\right) \ge \ln(0.9) \approx -0.10536,
\]  

\frac{t}{2N_e} \le 0.10536 \quad\Longrightarrow\quad N_e \ge \frac{t}{0.2107}.
\]  

For t = 1200: N_e \ge 5\,695.
For t = 10\,000: N_e \ge 47\,460.
With an N_e/N ratio of \approx 0.8 in age‑structured human populations, a census size of 40 000–50 000 is required. ∎

4.2 Loss of Heterozygosity (T2.2)

Statement.
Expected heterozygosity H_t decays exponentially as H_t = H_0 (1 - 1/(2N_e))^t. For N_e = 10\,000, H_{1200}/H_0 \approx 0.94 and H_{10\,000}/H_0 \approx 0.61. For N_e = 50\,000, the corresponding ratios are 0.99 and 0.90.

Proof.
In a neutral Wright–Fisher population, H_t = H_0 \left(1 - \frac{1}{2N_e}\right)^t \approx H_0 e^{-t/(2N_e)}. Substituting the values yields the stated figures. This quantifies the genetic erosion that must be counteracted. ∎

4.3 Gene Bank as a Migration Hedge (T2.3)

Statement.
A frozen embryo/gamete bank can be modelled as an external source of migrants at rate m per generation. The effective population size is inflated to approximately N_e' = N_e / (1-m)^2. Even a small migration rate can dramatically slow the loss of heterozygosity.

Proof sketch.
Island‑model migration increases effective size; for a single population receiving migrants from a genetically static bank, the expected heterozygosity at equilibrium is raised. A thaw rate of 50–100 individuals per generation out of 50 000 corresponds to m = 0.001\text{–}0.002. Full simulation (see Proposal 1) is required to confirm the exact preservation level, but the principle is well‑established in population genetics. ∎

