Chapter 5

Closed‑Loop Life Support: Impossibility of Sealed Permanence

5.1 Impossibility of Sealed Closure (T3.1)

Statement.
No physically realisable life‑support system can remain closed indefinitely. Even with a per‑cycle recycling efficiency \eta = 0.98 (the upper bound of MELISSA and BIOS‑3), the mass of a critical element decays to zero after a few thousand cycles. Perfect closure (\eta=1) violates the second law of thermodynamics.

Proof.
Let M_n be the mass of a life‑support element (e.g., oxygen) after n complete recycling cycles. Each cycle returns a fraction \eta of the material to the usable state:

M_n = M_0 \eta^n.
\]  

For \eta = 0.98 and n = 10\,000 (≈ 27 years at daily cycling for fast loops),

M_n/M_0 = 0.98^{10\,000} \approx e^{-202} \approx 10^{-88} \approx 0.
\]  

Even with \eta = 0.999, after 3 000 cycles only 0.999^{3000} \approx 0.049 remains—less than 5 % of the initial inventory. Thus, any claim of multi‑millennial closure without resupply is physically impossible. ∎

5.2 Resupply Requirement Theorem (T3.2)

Statement.
To maintain a steady‑state mass M of a life‑support loop, a continuous resupply influx I = M(1-\eta) is required per cycle. This reframes the vessel as an open industrial system with an onboard mining and processing capability.

Proof.
Mass balance with loss:

M_{t+1} = \eta M_t + I_t.
\]  

In steady state, M_{t+1} = M_t = M, so

M = \eta M + I \quad\Longrightarrow\quad I = M(1-\eta).
\]  

For water with \eta = 0.95 and a total inventory of 10^8 kg, the required make‑up is 5 \times 10^6 kg per cycle. This must be extracted from in‑situ resources (asteroidal or cometary material) during the voyage. ∎

5.3 Biosphere 2 Drift Lemma (T3.3)

Statement.
The observed oxygen decline in Biosphere 2 (≈0.3 % per day during the most severe phase, driven by unmodelled carbonation and soil respiration) provides an empirical lower bound for the rate of unanticipated sink accumulation in any large closed ecosystem. The assumption of zero hidden loss terms is demonstrably false.

Proof.
Published data from the 1991–1993 Biosphere 2 experiment show a monotonic decrease in O₂ concentration, primarily due to reaction with exposed concrete. This single experiment demonstrates that complex biological‑physical systems contain hidden mass sinks that cannot be fully predicted a priori. On a millennial timescale, such sinks will dominate unless continuous resupply and active management are employed. ∎

