Chapter 6

Governance Stability Across Deep Time

6.1 Immutability Decay (T4.1)

Statement.
No fixed constitution with a non‑zero per‑generation probability of amendment can survive arbitrarily many generations. If the probability that a given generation retains the original constitution without alteration is p < 1, then the survival probability after t generations is p^t, which tends to zero exponentially.

Proof.
Treat each generation’s decision as an independent Bernoulli trial with success probability p. The probability that the constitution remains identical to the founding version after t generations is p^t. For any p < 1, \lim_{t\to\infty} p^t = 0. Even for an extremely low amendment probability of 0.1 % per generation (p = 0.999), 0.999^{1200} \approx 0.30 and 0.999^{10\,000} \approx 4.5 \times 10^{-5}. Near‑immutable rigidity is a mathematical impossibility over millennial timescales. ∎

6.2 Two‑Layer Survival Condition (T4.2)

Statement.
A core set of survival‑critical rules (Layer A) can be protected against adversarial capture if (i) amendment requires a supermajority q \ge 0.75 and (ii) the rules are enforced by k \ge 5 independent, mutually vetoing subsystems. Under these conditions, the expected time to capture grows exponentially in k.

Proof sketch.
Model the system as a distributed Byzantine fault‑tolerant state machine. An adversary must simultaneously compromise a fraction > q of each of the k independent consensus groups to alter a Layer A rule. If the probability of compromising one group in a single generation is \epsilon, the probability of total capture is \epsilon^k. The expected time to capture is proportional to 1/\epsilon^k. For moderate \epsilon and k=5, this can comfortably exceed 10^5 generations. A full game‑theoretic formalisation is required (see Proposal 4). ∎

