Chapter 7

Knowledge and Culture Transmission

7.1 Telephone Game Bound (T5.1)

Statement.
If the per‑generation fidelity of social knowledge transmission is 1-\epsilon, with \epsilon = 0.01 (1 % loss per teaching generation), then after 1 200 generations the fidelity is (0.99)^{1200} \approx 6 \times 10^{-6}. Naïve social learning alone guarantees catastrophic loss of essential knowledge.

Proof.
Assume each generation independently introduces an error rate \epsilon; the fidelity after t generations is (1-\epsilon)^t. For \epsilon=0.01, t=1200, this is \approx 6.1 \times 10^{-6}. The same decay holds for any \epsilon > 0. ∎

7.2 Archive Re‑grounding Theorem (T5.2)

Statement.
If every G generations knowledge is re‑verified against a physical archive with fidelity 1-\delta, the effective fidelity becomes (1-\epsilon)^G (1-\delta) per re‑grounding cycle. To maintain an overall fidelity > 0.9 after 10 000 generations, one must re‑ground at intervals G \le 10\text{–}20 using archives with \delta < 10^{-4}.

Proof.
Over t generations with re‑grounding interval G, there are t/G transmission segments. After the first segment, fidelity is F_1 = (1-\epsilon)^G (1-\delta). After t/G repetitions,

F_t = \left[ (1-\epsilon)^G (1-\delta) \right]^{t/G}.
\]  

For t = 10\,000, \epsilon = 0.01, and G = 10, we have (1-\epsilon)^G \approx 0.904; to keep the per‑segment factor close to 1, \delta must be very small. If \delta = 10^{-4}, then F_t \approx (0.904 \times 0.9999)^{1000} \approx 0.90. Physical archives such as etched nickel, fused silica optical discs, or encapsulated DNA can achieve bit‑error rates far below 10^{-4} over millennia. Thus, a rigorous re‑grounding protocol every 10–20 generations is mandatory. ∎

