Chapter 8

Threat Detection and Structural Risk

8.1 Latency Floor Lemma (T6.1)

Statement.
No threat detection system can provide a response latency shorter than the round‑trip light‑travel time plus internal processing delay. The claim of “< 500 ms for existential threats” is physically impossible for any threat beyond a distance of 75 000 km.

Proof.
Let R be the distance to the threat, c the speed of light. Minimum latency \tau = 2R/c + t_{\text{process}}. Setting \tau = 0.5 s and ignoring processing time gives R \le \frac{0.5c}{2} = 74\,948 km. For a worldship with a detection horizon of millions of kilometres (necessary for high‑velocity debris avoidance), latency is measured in seconds to minutes. The flat claim is false. ∎

8.2 Sensor Horizon from 2026 Radar (T6.2)

Statement.
Using a radar with peak power P_t and antenna gain G, the maximum detection range for a debris object of radar cross‑section \sigma scales as R_{\max} \propto (P_t G^2 \sigma)^{1/4}. With an NEP‑derived power budget of ~10 MW for the radar, detection of 1 cm debris is limited to a few thousand kilometres.

Proof.
From the radar equation,

P_r = \frac{P_t G^2 \lambda^2 \sigma}{(4\pi)^3 R^4}.
\]  

For a minimum detectable signal P_{r,\min},

R_{\max} = \left( \frac{P_t G^2 \lambda^2 \sigma}{(4\pi)^3 P_{r,\min}} \right)^{1/4}.
\]  

Inserting realistic S‑band parameters (\lambda = 0.1 m, G \approx 50 dBi) and a 10 MW peak power, R_{\max} for a 1 cm² target is ≈ 2 000–5 000 km. This replaces any unsubstantiated claims of interstellar‑range detection. ∎

