Chapter 9

Cost Analysis: Non‑Linear Scaling of Complex Systems

9.1 ISS Scaling Fallacy (T7.1)

Statement.
The total system cost of a self‑sufficient worldship does not scale linearly with mass. Empirically, complex integrated systems follow a power law C \propto M^\gamma with \gamma > 1. Using ISS data and submarine/aircraft carrier analogues, \gamma \approx 1.3\text{–}1.8. For a 1.2 × 10⁶ t vessel, the cost lies in the range $1.8 × 10¹³ to $5 × 10¹⁴ (tens to hundreds of trillions of USD).

Proof.
The ISS cost ~$150 B for 450 t, giving ~$330 M t⁻¹, but only because of continuous resupply and the absence of self‑sufficiency. Historical data for submarines (e.g., a 20 000 t submarine costs far more than twice a 10 000 t boat) yield scaling exponents \gamma = 1.3\text{–}1.5. For a worldship with full life‑support closure, in‑situ mining, and multi‑millennial durability, integration complexity pushes \gamma toward the higher end. Applying

C_{\text{worldship}} = C_{\text{ISS}} \left( \frac{M_{\text{worldship}}}{M_{\text{ISS}}} \right)^\gamma
\]  

with M_{\text{worldship}} = 1.2 \times 10^6 t, M_{\text{ISS}} = 450 t, yields $18 trillion for \(\gamma=1.5\) and $500 trillion for \gamma=1.8. This represents a lower‑bound estimate pending a detailed bottom‑up model (Proposal 6). The original $1 trillion figure is at least one order of magnitude too low. ∎

