Chapter 10

Ethical and Legal Framework: The Intergenerational Consent Problem

The multi‑millennial timescale introduces a fundamental ethical challenge that no previous generation‑ship study has adequately addressed: no founding generation can morally bind the 1 200th generation to the voyage. Applying a Rawlsian “veil of ignorance,” a rational individual would not consent to be born into a closed, multi‑thousand‑generation interstellar journey with no possibility of exit, unless the expected quality of life and autonomy are at least equivalent to—and ideally better than—any available terrestrial alternative. This principle places a heavy burden on the governance and habitat design: the vessel must not merely keep people alive, but must provide a flourishing society, genuine political agency, and a credible path to eventual freedom (e.g., settlement of the destination or adaptation into a permanent deep‑space civilisation). The two‑layer governance model of Chapter 6 is a necessary but not sufficient condition for meeting this ethical obligation. The consent problem remains open for formal ethical and legal analysis, but it is an explicit constraint that must shape all further architectural decisions.

