Chapter 11

Synthesis: Decoupling the Mission — Phase I Oort Cloud Settlement

The formal results of Chapters 3–9 collectively compel a fundamental redefinition of the mission objective. The core findings are:

· Transit time to Proxima: 32 700–254 000 years (T1.2) — longer than Homo sapiens has existed.
· Minimum population: 40 000–50 000 (T2.1) to avoid genetic collapse.
· Life support: Cannot be closed; requires continuous asteroid mining (T3.1, T3.2).
· Governance: Any fixed constitution decays exponentially; only a two‑layer system with distributed veto has a chance of survival (T4.1, T4.2).
· Knowledge: Must be re‑grounded every 10–20 generations via physical archives (T5.2).
· Cost: Tens to hundreds of trillions of dollars (T7.1).

These are not engineering challenges that can be optimised away; they are mathematical consequences of the A_{2026} constraints. Therefore, an interstellar mission to Proxima Centauri is not a valid design target under 2026 technology.

However, the same theorems reveal an alternative that is achievable: a permanent deep‑space civilisation within our own solar system. Consider a Phase I Oort Cloud Settlement:

· Target orbit: 500–1 000 AU from the Sun.
· Required \Delta v: ≈ 15 km s⁻¹, well within the proven capability of NEP (T1.1).
· Transit time: 50–100 years, i.e., 2–4 generations. The genetics, closure, governance, and knowledge preservation problems become tractable: t \le 4 generations means F_t < 0.01 even with N_e = 500; recycling losses are manageable over decades; constitutional drift is minimal; and cultural continuity is straightforward.
· Resupply: The vessel can mine Kuiper Belt and Oort Cloud objects, closing the loop with a small industrial plant (Proposal 2).
· Cost: Still enormous, but within the lower end of the estimated range and spread over a century‑scale construction timeline.

This Phase I mission satisfies the original mandate of creating a resilient, distributed civilisation as a hedge against terrestrial existential risks. It also serves as the enabling infrastructure for a future interstellar voyage: if, and only if, subsequent research yields a propulsion breakthrough (e.g., fusion with I_{sp} > 50\,000 s, or beamed energy), a Phase II Proxima mission could be launched from the Oort Cloud settlement, using it as a proven deep‑space industrial base. But that decision belongs to future generations, not to us.

Thus, the Slow Ship Constitution is formally re‑defined as the architectural specification for a permanent deep‑space civilisation; the interstellar destination becomes a conditional annex, not the primary design driver.

