Chapter 12

Future Work: Closing the Remaining Gaps

The theorems presented in Chapters 3–9 establish hard bounds and necessary conditions, but several open problems remain. The following seven research proposals are designed to close these gaps using only technology within A_{2026}. They form the next phase of work to transform the Slow Ship Constitution from a theoretical framework into a fully validated engineering architecture.

Governing Principle from T1.2: The transit time to Proxima is 32 700–254 000 years. No proposal may optimise for speed to Proxima. All proposals optimise for Phase I, a resilient Oort Cloud / free‑floating civilisation.

---

Proposal 1: Population Genetics Simulator (SLOWGEN) — Close T2.1 / T2.3

Gap: T2.1 proves N_e \ge 47\,460 for F_t < 0.1 at t=10\,000, but uses an ideal Wright‑Fisher model. Real populations have age structure, variance in reproductive success, and are subject to catastrophic events.

Proposed work:
Build SLOWGEN, a Wright‑Fisher simulation with overlapping generations, a census population of N = 50\,000, and a carrying capacity that fluctuates stochastically by ±10 %. Run 100 Monte Carlo realisations over 10 000 generations under four conditions:
(a) no gene bank;
(b) gene bank with migration rates m = 0.001, 0.002, 0.005 per generation;
(c) periodic bottlenecks of 30 % population loss every 500 generations;
(d) combined gene bank and bottleneck scenarios.
Metrics for each run: inbreeding coefficient F_t, expected heterozygosity H_t/H_0, accumulation of deleterious mutational load L, and extinction probability.

Deliverable: A formal proof that a founding population of 40 000–50 000 with a gene bank operating at m = 0.002 keeps H_{10\,000}/H_0 > 0.85 and P(\text{extinction}) < 10^{-4}. If the simulation fails to meet these criteria, the minimum viable N must be revised upward. This is Priority 1.

---

Proposal 2: Open‑Loop Life Support Testbed — Close T3.1 / T3.2

Gap: T3.1 proves \eta=0.98 implies M_n \to 0. T3.2 proves the need for an influx I = M(1-\eta). No system has ever been operated with an integrated mining influx.

Proposed work:
Stop funding research aimed at “100 % closed” life support. Redefine the target as 98 % closure + 2 % mined resupply. Build a 2‑year ground testbed: couple an existing MELISSA‑type loop to a simulated asteroid regolith processor. The influx consists of crushed CI‑chondrite simulant, water ice, and nitrates, supplied at the rate I dictated by the measured loss fractions. Measure the real per‑cycle efficiencies \eta for O₂, H₂O, C, and N separately — T3.2 fails if any single critical element has \eta = 0.90 while the loop average is higher. Quantify the industrial mass (crusher, furnace, electrolyser) required to sustain I, and verify that this mass remains below 15 % of the habitat dry mass; otherwise the mass‑ratio bound R \le 5 in T1.1 may be violated.

Deliverable: Element‑specific resupply rates I_i and a proof that the industrial plant mass is within the structural budget. This directly replaces the fiction of a sealed terrarium with an open, maintainable system.

---

Proposal 3: Archive Physics — Close T5.2

Gap: T5.2 proves re‑grounding is mandatory every G = 10\text{–}20 generations, but assumes an archive fidelity \delta < 10^{-4}. No physical archive has been demonstrated to retain that fidelity over 10^4 years.

Proposed work:
Downselect three candidate archive formats that lie within A_{2026}: etched nickel (Nanofiche), fused silica 5D optical data storage, and dry DNA encapsulated in silica. Subject samples to accelerated ageing: thermal cycling, vacuum, and ionising radiation equivalent to a 10 000‑year deep‑space exposure. Measure the bit‑error rate (BER) for each format and compute an empirical \delta. Use the measured \delta in the T5.2 formula to recalculate the required re‑grounding interval G. Design a Re‑grounding Protocol: every G-th generation must physically read the primary archive, pass a standardised competency examination, and rewrite a fresh working copy. Failure to complete this protocol triggers an automatic halt of all cultural‑drift procedures and invokes Layer‑A governance override.

Deliverable: A measured \delta for each format and a validated G. If \delta > 10^{-4} for all formats, then G must be less than 5 generations, and the mission may be culturally unviable — a critical go/no‑go criterion.

---

Proposal 4: Governance Stress Test via Agent‑Based Modelling — Close T4.1 / T4.2

Gap: T4.1 proves that any fixed constitution with p<1 decays as p^t. T4.2 claims that a two‑layer system with k=5 independent veto bodies yields exponentially long capture times, but this remains a proof sketch.

Proposed work:
Construct an agent‑based model of the worldship society: 50 000 agents, evolved over 1 200 generations. In each generation, agents may attempt to amend Layer‑A rules with probability p_{\text{amend}} = 0.005. Test two governance architectures:

· A1: A single council, supermajority q=0.75.
· A2: Five independent, mutually vetoing subsystems — Life Support, Navigation, Genome, Archive, and Justice — each requiring q=0.75 for any amendment.
  Introduce adversarial perturbations: resource scarcity crises, ideological cascades, and concentrated power grabs. Measure the time to first successful capture of a Layer‑A rule (e.g., unilateral control of life support). Run sufficient Monte Carlo trials to bound the expected capture time E[T_{\text{capture}}].
  Deliverable: A proof that E[T_{\text{capture}}] for A2 exceeds 100 000 generations while A1 falls below 400 generations. This would elevate the two‑layer governance model from a plausible assertion to a formal theorem.

---

Proposal 5: Sensor Horizon Prototype — Close T6.1 / T6.2

Gap: T6.1 proves the sub‑500 ms latency claim is false. T6.2 gives a theoretical R_{\max} \propto (P_t G^2 \sigma)^{1/4}, but uses paper values.

Proposed work:
Build a 10 MW‑peak S‑band phased‑array radar using commercial off‑the‑shelf (COTS) components within A_{2026}. Conduct field tests against calibrated targets of 1 cm, 10 cm, and 1 m radar cross‑section at ranges of 1 000–5 000 km. Publish the measured R_{\max}(\sigma) curve and the actual latency function \tau(R) = 2R/c + t_{\text{process}}.
