Chapter 13

Conclusion

The Slow Ship Constitution began as a critique of the original Vaisseau‑Monde concept and has evolved into a rigorous, mathematically grounded architectural framework for deep‑space civilisation. By enforcing a strict 2026 Admissible Technology Set, we have eliminated the most persistent fallacies that have haunted interstellar‑vessel design. We have proven that any nuclear‑electric worldship is velocity‑limited to 5–39 km s⁻¹, yielding transit times to Proxima Centauri on the order of 30 000 to 250 000 years. We have derived the minimum viable founding population—40 000 to 50 000—required to preserve genetic health over 1 200–10 000 generations. We have shown that closed‑loop life support is a physical impossibility and that the vessel must be an open industrial system with continuous asteroid mining. We have proved that no fixed constitution can survive the exponential decay of immutability, and that only a two‑layer governance architecture with distributed veto can plausibly endure. We have quantified the information‑theoretic necessity of physical archive re‑grounding, the true physical limits of threat detection, and the non‑linear cost scaling that pushes the price of such a vessel into the tens to hundreds of trillions of dollars.

Taken together, these results make a compelling case that the original Proxima‑bound worldship is not a valid design target under 2026 technology. But they also open a door to a more honest and achievable vision: a Phase I Oort Cloud settlement that fulfils the civilisational resilience mandate, provides a testbed for the deep‑time technologies we will need, and leaves the door open for future generations to make the interstellar leap if and when the physics permits.

This dissertation has laid the mathematical spine and defined the open problems. The seven proposals of Chapter 12 constitute the research programme that must now be executed to turn the Slow Ship Constitution from a set of proven bounds into a fully validated engineering specification. If we are serious about building a backup for humanity, the work starts here.


Appendix A. Mathematical Preliminaries

This appendix summarises the principal mathematical frameworks employed in the dissertation. All notation and definitions introduced here are used throughout the proofs that follow.

A.1 Rocket Propulsion

The ideal rocket equation (Tsiolkovsky 1903) for a vehicle of initial mass m_0 and final mass m_f that expels propellant with constant effective exhaust velocity v_e reads

\Delta v = v_e \ln\frac{m_0}{m_f} = v_e \ln R, \qquad R \equiv \frac{m_0}{m_f}. \tag{A.1}

The exhaust velocity is related to the specific impulse I_{sp} by v_e = g_0 I_{sp} with g_0 = 9.80665\ \text{m s}^{-2}. For electric propulsion, the jet power P_{jet} required to produce thrust T is

P_{jet} = \frac{1}{2} \dot m v_e^2 = \frac{T v_e}{2\eta}, \tag{A.2}
\]  

where \eta is the thruster efficiency.

A.2 Population Genetics

We consider a diploid, randomly mating population of constant census size N and effective size N_e. Generations are discrete. The inbreeding coefficient F_t is the probability that two homologous alleles are identical by descent in generation t; it satisfies the recurrence

F_t = \frac{1}{2N_e} + \left(1 - \frac{1}{2N_e}\right) F_{t-1}, \qquad F_0 = 0. \tag{A.3}

The solution is

F_t = 1 - \left(1 - \frac{1}{2N_e}\right)^t. \tag{A.4}

Expected heterozygosity H_t (the fraction of heterozygous loci in a neutral model) decays according to

H_t = H_0 \left(1 - \frac{1}{2N_e}\right)^t. \tag{A.5}

When a fraction m of the population is replaced each generation by immigrants drawn from a genetically constant source (e.g., a frozen gene bank), the effective population size is inflated. For an island model with one-way migration,

N_e' \approx \frac{N_e}{(1-m)^2}, \tag{A.6}
\]  

valid for small m.

A.3 Exponential Decay and Approximations

For |x| \ll 1, the expansions \ln(1-x) \approx -x - \frac{x^2}{2} - \cdots and (1-x)^t \approx \exp(-xt) are used. In most proofs the first-order term suffices.

A.4 Information Transmission

We model the propagation of a corpus of knowledge as a binary symmetric channel that introduces an error probability \epsilon per generation. The fidelity (probability that any given unit of information is correct after t generations of uncorrected social transmission) is

\mathcal{F}_t = (1-\epsilon)^t. \tag{A.7}

If, at intervals of G generations, the active knowledge set is reset by reading a physical archive with per-read error probability \delta, the fidelity after a reset is restored to 1-\delta. Consequently, after G generations of subsequent social transmission the fidelity becomes (1-\delta)(1-\epsilon)^G. The minimum fidelity observed during any inter‑reset period is therefore bounded by this value.

A.5 Radar Equation

For a monostatic radar transmitting peak power P_t, with antenna gain G, wavelength \lambda, and target radar cross‑section \sigma, the received power is

P_r = \frac{P_t G^2 \lambda^2 \sigma}{(4\pi)^3 R^4}. \tag{A.8}
\]  

If the minimum detectable signal is P_{r,\min}, the maximum detection range is

R_{\max} = \left(\frac{P_t G^2 \lambda^2 \sigma}{(4\pi)^3 P_{r,\min}}\right)^{\!1/4}. \tag{A.9}

A.6 Power‑Law Cost Scaling

Empirical data for complex, self‑sufficient engineering systems show that total cost C scales with system mass M as

C \propto M^\gamma, \qquad \gamma > 1, \tag{A.10}
\]  

reflecting increasing integration complexity. Analogues (submarines, aircraft carriers, chemical plants) suggest \gamma \in [1.3, 1.8].

