Appendix B. Proofs for Propulsion and the Velocity Ceiling (Chapter 3)

B.1 Velocity Ceiling Lemma (T1.1)

Theorem B.1 (Velocity Ceiling).
Under the admissible technology set A_{2026}, the total mission \Delta v of a nuclear‑electric worldship is bounded above by 79.0\ \text{km s}^{-1}, and the attainable steady cruise velocity is confined to the interval [5, 39]\ \text{km s}^{-1}.

Proof.
From A_{2026} the specific impulse satisfies I_{sp} \in [2500, 5000]\ \text{s}, giving an exhaust velocity

v_e = g_0 I_{sp} \in [9.80665 \times 2500,\; 9.80665 \times 5000] = [24.5, 49.0]\ \text{km s}^{-1}.
\]  

The permissible mass ratio is R = m_0/m_f \in [1.5, 5.0]. Using the ideal rocket equation (A.1),

\Delta v = v_e \ln R.
\]  

Substituting the extrema,

\Delta v_{\min} = 24.5 \cdot \ln(1.5) \approx 9.94\ \text{km s}^{-1},\qquad
\Delta v_{\max} = 49.0 \cdot \ln(5.0) \approx 78.95\ \text{km s}^{-1}.
\]  

A fraction \kappa \in [0.5, 0.8] of \Delta v is available for the cruise phase after gravity losses, mid‑course corrections, and terminal deceleration. Hence

v_{\text{cruise}} = \kappa \Delta v \in [0.5 \times 9.94,\; 0.8 \times 78.95] \approx [5.0,\; 39.0]\ \text{km s}^{-1}.
\]  

The upper bound on R follows from structural mass constraints (see Chapter 5): the dry mass includes a massive habitat, radiation shielding, and life‑support plant. A mass ratio R > 5 would require a propellant mass exceeding four times the dry mass; with 2026 structural materials the required tankage and support structure would violate the admissible mass fraction. ∎

B.2 Transit Time Theorem (T1.2)

Theorem B.2 (Transit Time).
For a voyage to Proxima Centauri at distance D = 4.01 \times 10^{13}\ \text{km}, the one‑way transit time satisfies

T \in [32\,700,\; 254\,000]\ \text{years}.

Proof.
Using the cruise velocity bounds from Theorem B.1,

T = \frac{D}{v_{\text{cruise}}} \quad\Longrightarrow\quad 
T_{\min} = \frac{4.01 \times 10^{13}\ \text{km}}{39.0\ \text{km s}^{-1}} \approx 1.03 \times 10^{12}\ \text{s} \approx 32\,700\ \text{years},
\]  

T_{\max} = \frac{4.01 \times 10^{13}\ \text{km}}{5.0\ \text{km s}^{-1}} \approx 8.02 \times 10^{12}\ \text{s} \approx 254\,000\ \text{years},
\]  

where 1\ \text{year} = 3.156 \times 10^7\ \text{s}. The acceleration and deceleration phases, lasting decades at the available MW–GW power levels, are negligible relative to the cruise duration. ∎

B.3 Open Problem T1.3 – Multi‑Burn Trajectories

Statement. Does there exist a sequence of n propulsive manoeuvres, possibly using pre‑placed fuel depots or gravity assists, that yields an effective hyperbolic excess speed exceeding v_e \ln R under the constraints \sum_i m_{\text{prop},i} \le M_{\text{fuel}} and P \le P_{\max}?

A formal variational problem can be stated:

\text{Maximise}\quad v_{\text{arr}} = \left\| \sum_{i=1}^n \Delta \mathbf{v}_i \right\| \quad\text{subject to}\quad
\sum_{i=1}^n \left[ m_{0,i} \left(1 - e^{-\Delta v_i / v_e}\right) \right] \le M_{\text{fuel}},\quad
P_i \le P_{\max},
\]  

and the trajectory satisfies Newtonian dynamics in the local galactic potential. No solution has been demonstrated that increases v_{\text{arr}} beyond the single‑burn bound within A_{2026}; the problem is left open for future trajectory optimisation studies.

