Appendix C. Proofs for Population Genetics (Chapter 4)

C.1 Minimum Founder Bound (T2.1)

Theorem C.1 (Founder Size Requirement).
To maintain an inbreeding coefficient F_t < 0.1 after t generations of isolation, the effective population size must satisfy N_e \ge \dfrac{t}{2\ln\!\big( (1-0.1)^{-1/t} \big)}. For t = 10\,000, this yields N_e \ge 47\,500; a founding census size of 40\,000–50\,000 is therefore required under realistic N_e/N ratios.

Proof.
From (A.4) we require F_t < 0.1, i.e.,

1 - \left(1 - \frac{1}{2N_e}\right)^t < 0.1 \quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\left(1 - \frac{1}{2N_e}\right)^t > 0.9.

Taking natural logarithms,

t \ln\!\left(1 - \frac{1}{2N_e}\right) > \ln 0.9 \approx -0.10536.

For large N_e we may expand the logarithm to first order, \ln(1 - 1/(2N_e)) \approx -1/(2N_e), giving

-\frac{t}{2N_e} \gtrsim -0.10536 \quad\Longrightarrow\quad N_e \gtrsim \frac{t}{0.21072} \approx 47\,460 \ \text{for } t=10\,000.

A more exact solution is obtained by solving the strict inequality:

N_e > \frac{1}{2\Bigl[1 - (0.9)^{1/t}\Bigr]}.
\]  

For t=10\,000, (0.9)^{1/10000} \approx 0.99998947, giving N_e > 47\,500. With a typical N_e/N ratio of 0.8 in age‑structured human populations, the census size must be at least 40\,000–50\,000. ∎

C.2 Heterozygosity Decay (T2.2)

Theorem C.2 (Heterozygosity Loss).
The expected heterozygosity after t generations is H_t = H_0 \bigl(1 - \frac{1}{2N_e}\bigr)^t. For N_e = 10\,000, the fraction retained is 0.94 at t=1200 and 0.61 at t=10\,000; for N_e = 50\,000, the corresponding fractions are 0.99 and 0.90.

Proof.
Equation (A.5) follows directly from the neutral Wright–Fisher model: each generation multiplies the heterozygosity by the probability that two alleles do not coalesce in the previous generation, namely 1 - 1/(2N_e). Substituting the numerical values yields the stated decay ratios. ∎

C.3 Gene Bank as a Migration Hedge (T2.3)

Theorem C.3 (Migration Inflation of Effective Size).
If a fraction m of the population is replaced each generation by individuals derived from a frozen, genetically diverse bank, the effective population size is approximately N_e' \approx N_e / (1-m)^2, and the asymptotic decay rate of heterozygosity is correspondingly reduced.

Proof.
Under the standard island model with one‑way migration from a large source of constant allele frequencies, the effective size is

N_e' \approx \frac{N_e}{1 - m^2}
\]  

for small m. However, if the migrants are drawn from the initial gene pool (as is the case for a thawed embryo bank), a more precise expression based on the inbreeding effective size gives N_e' \approx N_e / (1-m)^2 (see Wang & Caballero 1999). With m = 0.002, the effective size is increased by a factor \approx 1.004; the principal benefit is the reintroduction of lost alleles, which arrests the fixation of deleterious variants and greatly reduces the probability of extinction (to be quantified by the SLOWGEN simulations in Proposal 1). ∎

