Appendix D. Proofs for Closed‑Loop Life Support (Chapter 5)

D.1 Impossibility of Sealed Closure (T3.1)

Theorem D.1 (No Perfect Closure).
For any finite per‑cycle recycling efficiency \eta < 1, the inventory of a critical consumable decays to zero. Perfect closure (\eta = 1) is forbidden by the second law of thermodynamics.

Proof.
Let M_n denote the mass of the resource after n full cycles. If each cycle returns a fraction \eta to the usable state,

M_n = \eta M_{n-1} = \eta^n M_0.
\]  

For \eta = 0.98 and n = 10\,000 (equivalent to \sim 27 years of daily cycling),

M_{10\,000} = M_0 \times 0.98^{10\,000} \approx M_0 \times 1.87 \times 10^{-88} \approx 0.
\]  

Even with \eta = 0.999, after n = 3\,000 cycles one finds 0.999^{3000} \approx 0.0497, i.e., less than 5\% of the initial inventory remains. Because achieving \eta = 1 would imply zero entropy production—a violation of the second law—no physical system can sustain indefinite closure. ∎

D.2 Resupply Requirement (T3.2)

Theorem D.2 (Steady‑State Make‑Up).
To maintain a constant inventory M in the presence of loss fraction 1-\eta, a per‑cycle make‑up influx I = M(1-\eta) must be supplied.

Proof.
Mass balance over one cycle:

M_{t+1} = \eta M_t + I_t.
\]  

Imposing steady state M_{t+1} = M_t = M and constant influx I_t = I gives

M = \eta M + I \quad\Longrightarrow\quad I = M(1-\eta).
\]  

For a water inventory of 10^8\ \text{kg} and \eta = 0.95, the required influx is 5 \times 10^6\ \text{kg cycle}^{-1}. ∎

D.3 Biosphere 2 Drift Lemma (T3.3)

Lemma D.3 (Empirical Leak Rate).
The documented oxygen decline in Biosphere 2 demonstrates that unmodeled sinks are inevitable in complex closed ecosystems, providing a lower bound for the drift rate.

Proof.
During the 1991–1993 closure, atmospheric O₂ fell from 21% to below 14% due to unanticipated carbonation of concrete and soil respiration. This monotonic trend shows that even state‑of‑the‑art biospheric engineering cannot pre‑dict all loss terms. For a multi‑millennial vessel, continuous monitoring and external resupply are mandatory. ∎

