Appendix E. Proofs for Governance Stability (Chapter 6)

E.1 Immutability Decay (T4.1)

Theorem E.1 (Constitutional Drift).
If the probability that a single generation retains the founding constitution unamended is p < 1, then the probability that it survives unchanged for t generations is p^t, which tends to 0 exponentially.

Proof.
Model the amendment process as a sequence of independent Bernoulli trials with success probability p. The joint probability of t consecutive successes is p^t. For any p < 1, \lim_{t\to\infty} p^t = 0. For p = 0.999 and t=10\,000, p^t \approx 4.5 \times 10^{-5}, confirming that immutable governance is mathematically impossible on the timescale of interest. ∎

E.2 Two‑Layer Survival Condition (T4.2)

Theorem E.2 (Distributed Veto Protection).
Consider a set of k mutually independent consensus groups, each requiring a supermajority q \ge 0.75 to amend a protected rule. If the per‑generation probability of an adversary capturing a single group is \epsilon \ll 1, the expected time to capture the entire Layer A is E[T] \approx 1/\epsilon^k, which grows exponentially in k.

Proof.
Capture of the complete set of rules requires simultaneous control of all k groups. Assuming independence, the probability of total capture in a given generation is \epsilon^k. The number of generations until the first success follows a geometric distribution with success probability \epsilon^k; its expected value is 1/\epsilon^k. For \epsilon = 0.01 and k = 5, E[T] = 10^{10} generations, far exceeding the mission duration. This scaling holds so long as the independence and quorum requirements are maintained. ∎

