Appendix F. Proofs for Knowledge Transmission (Chapter 7)

F.1 Telephone Game Bound (T5.1)

Theorem F.1 (Error Accumulation).
If each generation transmits a body of knowledge with per‑item fidelity 1-\epsilon, then after t generations of uncorrected copying the fidelity is (1-\epsilon)^t. For \epsilon = 0.01 and t = 1\,200, this is \approx 6\times 10^{-6}.

Proof.
Each generation independently introduces error probability \epsilon. The probability that a particular fact is correctly preserved across t independent transmission steps is (1-\epsilon)^t. Direct substitution yields the numerical value. ∎

F.2 Archive Re‑grounding Theorem (T5.2) – Corrected Formulation

Theorem F.2 (Re‑grounding Requirement).
Let the active knowledge base be reset every G generations by reading a physical archive with per‑read error probability \delta. During the subsequent G generations, social transmission degrades fidelity by a factor (1-\epsilon)^G. To guarantee that the worst‑case fidelity never falls below a critical threshold \mathcal{F}_{\min}, the reset interval must satisfy

(1-\delta)(1-\epsilon)^G \ge \mathcal{F}_{\min}.
\]  

For \epsilon = 0.01, \delta = 10^{-4}, and \mathcal{F}_{\min} = 0.9, this gives G \le 10.

Proof.
Immediately after a reset, the knowledge base reflects the archive with fidelity 1-\delta (the archive may suffer a read error). Over the next G generations of social transmission, the fidelity is multiplied by (1-\epsilon)^G (Theorem F.1). Hence, the fidelity just before the next reset—the worst point in the cycle—is \mathcal{F}_{\text{min,cycle}} = (1-\delta)(1-\epsilon)^G. For the overall mission to maintain a minimum fidelity \mathcal{F}_{\min} at all times, we require this value to be at least \mathcal{F}_{\min}.

Solving for G:

G \le \frac{\ln\!\big(\mathcal{F}_{\min} / (1-\delta)\big)}{\ln(1-\epsilon)}.

With \mathcal{F}_{\min}=0.9, \delta=10^{-4}, and \epsilon=0.01,

G \le \frac{\ln(0.9 / 0.9999)}{\ln(0.99)} \approx \frac{-0.10536}{-0.01005} \approx 10.5.
\]  

Thus, G \le 10 is a safe bound. If the archive error rate \delta is higher, the permissible interval shortens accordingly. ∎

