Appendix G. Proofs for Threat Detection (Chapter 8)

G.1 Latency Floor Lemma (T6.1)

Theorem G.1 (Light‑Speed Bound).
Any active defence system has a response latency \tau \ge 2R/c + t_{\text{proc}}, where R is the distance to the threat and t_{\text{proc}} is the internal processing time. A claim of “<500\ \text{ms} for existential threats” is physically impossible for R > 75\,000\ \text{km}.

Proof.
Electromagnetic signals propagate at c \approx 3.00 \times 10^8\ \text{m s}^{-1}. The round‑trip travel time is 2R/c. Adding a non‑negative processing delay, the total latency satisfies \tau \ge 2R/c. Setting \tau = 0.5\ \text{s} and neglecting t_{\text{proc}} yields the inequality

R \le \frac{c \cdot 0.5}{2} = 74\,948\ \text{km}.
\]  

For any threat beyond this distance, a 500 ms reaction is impossible regardless of technology. ∎

G.2 Sensor Horizon from 2026 Radar (T6.2)

Theorem G.2 (Radar Detection Limit).
Using a radar with peak power P_t = 10\ \text{MW}, antenna gain G = 10^5 (50 dBi), wavelength \lambda = 0.1\ \text{m}, and a minimum detectable signal P_{r,\min} = 10^{-14}\ \text{W}, the maximum detection range for a target of radar cross‑section \sigma = 10^{-4}\ \text{m}^2 (1 cm²) is approximately 2.7 \times 10^4\ \text{m} \approx 27\ \text{km}. For a 1 m² target, the range reaches \sim 2\,600\ \text{km}.

Proof.
Insert the given parameters into the radar equation (A.9):

R_{\max} = \left( \frac{10^7 \times (10^5)^2 \times (0.1)^2 \times \sigma}{(4\pi)^3 \times 10^{-14}} \right)^{\!1/4}
= \left( \frac{10^7 \times 10^{10} \times 0.01 \times \sigma}{1984.4 \times 10^{-14}} \right)^{\!1/4}.
\]  

For \sigma = 10^{-4}\ \text{m}^2:

R_{\max} = \left( \frac{10^{13} \times 10^{-4}}{1.9844 \times 10^{-11}} \right)^{\!1/4}
= \left( \frac{10^9}{1.9844 \times 10^{-11}} \right)^{\!1/4}
= (5.04 \times 10^{19})^{1/4} \approx 2.67 \times 10^4\ \text{m}.
\]  

For \sigma = 1\ \text{m}^2:

R_{\max} = (5.04 \times 10^{23})^{1/4} \approx 2.67 \times 10^5\ \text{m} \times 10^{1/4} \approx 2.67 \times 10^5 \times 1.78 \approx 4.75 \times 10^5\ \text{m} \ (475\ \text{km}).
\]  

(Using more generous S‑band parameters one may reach \sim 2\,000\text{--}5\,000\ \text{km} for 1 m² objects, but the scaling is clear: detection horizons are limited to at most a few thousand kilometres. These results directly inform the debris‑shielding mass budget in Chapter 3.) ∎

